Functional Constipation
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Infrequent hard/lumpy stools, Excessive severe straining, Sensation of incomplete evacuation, Normal structural colonoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-014
Reason for Visit: Severe, localized lower left quadrant abdominal pain and intense distress due to absolute inability to pass stool for 5 days.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with escalating, severe lower abdominal pain and a multi-day history of absolute constipation, causing profound discomfort and functional limitation. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out a mechanical bowel obstruction, complicated diverticulitis, or an acute surgical abdomen via hands-on clinical examination, allowing safe in-home treatment prior to daytime GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower stomach is cramping so badly, I sit on the toilet for an hour straining until I cry, but it feels like there is a concrete block there."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 5-day history of infrequent bowel movements. When successful, the passage consists of hard, dry pellet-like stools (Bristol Stool Form Scale type 1). The patient describes excessive, painful, and intense straining required to initiate defecation, leaving a constant subjective sensation of incomplete rectal evacuation and severe anorectal obstruction. The patient explicitly denies overt rectal bleeding, nocturnal symptoms, fevers, or severe unintentional weight loss. Complete lack of loose stools without laxative use distinguishes this from IBS.
Functional Status: Mobile, but frequently retreating to the bathroom and bending over in discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme straining poses a risk of vasovagal syncope; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the lower left abdomen, frequently shifting weight.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft and non-distended globally. Palpable, firm, non-tender faecal loading is explicitly noted in the left lower quadrant (descending colon). Normal, active bowel sounds present in all four quadrants. Complete absence of rebound tenderness, rigidity, or localized peritonism. Digital rectal examination reveals hard stool in the rectal vault; no palpable masses or strictures. Documented normal colonoscopy 2 years prior.
Environmental Observations: Bathroom contains multiple over-the-counter stimulant laxative empty blister packs.
Medication Reconciliation: Chronic use of Senna with decreasing efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Functional Constipation - Status: Acute faecal impaction
Differentials:

Acute Diverticulitis - Ruled out due to absence of fever, completely soft abdomen without localized peritonism or guarding in the LLQ.

Mechanical Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out due to presence of active bowel sounds, flat/non-distended abdomen, and lack of faeculent vomiting.

Colorectal Carcinoma - Highly unlikely given the documented normal structural colonoscopy 2 years ago and absence of alarm symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to a severe, acute presentation of abdominal pain mimicking an acute surgical crisis (such as obstruction or diverticulitis) secondary to functional constipation. Immediate physical examination, including abdominal palpation and a digital rectal exam, was critical to definitively rule out peritonism and confirm simple faecal loading. Diagnosing the benign nature of the obstruction allowed for the immediate administration of targeted, safe enema therapy to relieve the acute pain crisis, safely preventing a highly distressed and unnecessary presentation to the emergency department. This fulfills the requirement for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a Microlax stat enema with good clinical effect (evacuation of hard stool). Prescribed Macrogol 3350 (Movicol) 1 sachet daily as maintenance osmotic therapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: Discontinue the use of harsh stimulant laxatives and replace them with a daily morning routine of drinking a large glass of warm water, followed immediately by a 10-minute walk. The physical activity and warm fluid will naturally stimulate the gastrocolic reflex, rewarding the patient with easier, spontaneous bowel movements without painful cramping.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Footstool for toilet posture.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to adjust the long-term osmotic laxative dose.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high fever, sudden unremitting and severe abdominal pain, vomiting of green or dark material, or passage of large amounts of bright red blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute relief of an impaction does not resolve the underlying functional dysmotility; adherence to a maintenance bowel regimen is strictly required.
Clinical Course: Functional constipation is a chronic biomechanical issue. Utilizing osmotic agents to keep the stool soft, rather than forcing the bowel to contract forcefully against a hard blockage, is the key to painless resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if your abdomen becomes suddenly rigid and extremely painful to touch, or if you start vomiting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Stimulant laxatives (like Senna) cause your bowel to spasm forcefully, which is agonizing when the stool is as hard as a rock. The new medication (Macrogol) pulls water into the bowel to soften the stool, allowing it to pass smoothly without painful cramping.
Actionable Steps: Take the Macrogol sachet every morning with a large glass of water. Observing the transition to soft, easy-to-pass stool will reinforce the absolute necessity of daily hydration and osmotic support over harsh stimulants.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Functional Constipation. Enema given with good relief. No surgical issues. Start daily Movicol. Stop Senna. See your regular GP in 1 week.

SMS EDUCATION

Take Movicol daily with plenty of water. Use a footstool on the toilet. Go to ED immediately if you get a high fever, severe constant pain, or start vomiting.
